1
00:00:00,520 --> 00:00:02,795
Sir, field report number three on the electricity

2
00:00:02,835 --> 00:00:03,216
file.

3
00:00:03,256 --> 00:00:03,802
Today:

4
00:00:03,842 --> 00:00:05,200
why the lights go out.

5
00:00:05,261 --> 00:00:06,688
Last time we learned the grid is a

6
00:00:06,728 --> 00:00:07,443
balance:

7
00:00:07,483 --> 00:00:10,556
what's made must equal what's used, every second.

8
00:00:10,596 --> 00:00:11,070
Today:

9
00:00:11,110 --> 00:00:12,862
what happens when the balance breaks.

10
00:00:12,902 --> 00:00:13,943
Strangest thing I found:

11
00:00:13,983 --> 00:00:16,281
in two thousand three, fifty million people in

12
00:00:16,321 --> 00:00:18,227
America and Canada sat in the dark.

13
00:00:18,267 --> 00:00:18,785
The cause:

14
00:00:18,825 --> 00:00:19,343
a tree..

15
00:00:19,383 --> 00:00:20,961
and a frozen computer program.

16
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:22,689
I'll tell you how a small fault grows,

17
00:00:22,729 --> 00:00:25,093
their biggest blackouts, the grid's enemies, and how

18
00:00:25,133 --> 00:00:27,306
a whole country climbs out of the dark.

19
00:00:27,346 --> 00:00:28,257
This is a grid:

20
00:00:28,297 --> 00:00:30,152
stations, cities, and lines between them.

21
00:00:30,192 --> 00:00:32,075
The power is shared across all the lines.

22
00:00:32,115 --> 00:00:33,104
One line trips.

23
00:00:33,144 --> 00:00:33,943
For any reason.

24
00:00:33,983 --> 00:00:35,850
The load it carried doesn't vanish.

25
00:00:35,890 --> 00:00:37,470
It moves onto the lines next to it.

26
00:00:37,510 --> 00:00:38,909
They carry more than they can..

27
00:00:38,949 --> 00:00:39,872
and trip too.

28
00:00:39,912 --> 00:00:40,673
And the next.

29
00:00:40,713 --> 00:00:41,422
And the next.

30
00:00:41,462 --> 00:00:44,231
In minutes, sometimes seconds, a whole region goes

31
00:00:44,271 --> 00:00:44,769
dark.

32
00:00:44,809 --> 00:00:46,762
Like workers carrying a heavy pole.

33
00:00:46,802 --> 00:00:47,587
One lets go..

34
00:00:47,627 --> 00:00:48,776
the weight falls on the rest.

35
00:00:48,816 --> 00:00:50,154
The next one can't take it, and lets

36
00:00:50,194 --> 00:00:50,759
go too.

37
00:00:50,799 --> 00:00:51,427
And so on.

38
00:00:51,467 --> 00:00:52,153
The strange part:

39
00:00:52,193 --> 00:00:54,432
what trips these lines isn't the fault itself.

40
00:00:54,472 --> 00:00:55,383
It's the protection.

41
00:00:55,423 --> 00:00:57,141
Every line and station has a device that

42
00:00:57,181 --> 00:00:58,587
cuts it off before it burns.

43
00:00:58,627 --> 00:01:00,264
Each piece saves itself..

44
00:01:00,304 --> 00:01:01,606
and the whole thing falls.

45
00:01:01,646 --> 00:01:02,671
Very human.

46
00:01:03,018 --> 00:01:03,861
Back to the tree.

47
00:01:03,901 --> 00:01:06,092
August two thousand three, a hot day in

48
00:01:06,132 --> 00:01:06,873
Ohio.

49
00:01:06,913 --> 00:01:08,807
Hot wires stretch and sag.

50
00:01:08,847 --> 00:01:10,852
A high-voltage line sagged until it touched a

51
00:01:10,892 --> 00:01:12,265
tree nobody had trimmed.

52
00:01:12,305 --> 00:01:13,064
And tripped.

53
00:01:13,104 --> 00:01:14,519
Normally that's fixed in minutes.

54
00:01:14,559 --> 00:01:16,325
But the alarm software in the control room

55
00:01:16,365 --> 00:01:18,878
had a bug, and stopped without anyone noticing.

56
00:01:18,918 --> 00:01:20,794
So the engineers sat at screens saying:

57
00:01:20,834 --> 00:01:21,460
all fine.

58
00:01:21,500 --> 00:01:23,339
Within two hours the dominoes fell.

59
00:01:23,379 --> 00:01:26,060
Fifty million people in America and Canada, all

60
00:01:26,100 --> 00:01:27,250
of New York City among them.

61
00:01:27,290 --> 00:01:29,408
Some places waited two days for the lights.

62
00:01:29,448 --> 00:01:31,177
The subway stopped with people in the tunnels,

63
00:01:31,217 --> 00:01:32,771
and people walked home over the bridges.

64
00:01:32,811 --> 00:01:34,837
The losses ran into billions of dollars.

65
00:01:34,877 --> 00:01:36,266
Priciest tree on Earth.

66
00:01:36,306 --> 00:01:38,610
Their biggest blackout ever was in India, July

67
00:01:38,650 --> 00:01:39,394
twenty twelve.

68
00:01:39,434 --> 00:01:40,247
The grid fell..

69
00:01:40,287 --> 00:01:41,229
they brought it back..

70
00:01:41,269 --> 00:01:42,652
and the next day it fell bigger.

71
00:01:42,692 --> 00:01:43,311
The cause:

72
00:01:43,351 --> 00:01:45,350
a hot summer, states drawing more than their

73
00:01:45,390 --> 00:01:47,577
share, lines carrying more than they could.

74
00:01:47,617 --> 00:01:49,262
On the second day, the dark covered an

75
00:01:49,302 --> 00:01:51,827
area with over six hundred million people.

76
00:01:51,867 --> 00:01:55,032
Trains stopped mid-track, and miners were trapped underground

77
00:01:55,072 --> 00:01:56,067
until they were pulled out.

78
00:01:56,107 --> 00:01:57,570
About half the country..

79
00:01:57,610 --> 00:01:58,248
at once.

80
00:01:58,288 --> 00:02:00,586
Not every blackout is a mistake.

81
00:02:00,626 --> 00:02:04,034
February twenty twenty-one, an ice storm hit Texas.

82
00:02:04,074 --> 00:02:06,183
Power plants and gas lines froze..

83
00:02:06,223 --> 00:02:08,569
just as everyone switched on their heaters.

84
00:02:08,671 --> 00:02:09,753
The balance tipped.

85
00:02:09,793 --> 00:02:11,249
So the control room did the only thing

86
00:02:11,289 --> 00:02:11,705
left:

87
00:02:11,745 --> 00:02:13,822
it cut power by hand to over four

88
00:02:13,862 --> 00:02:14,652
million homes.

89
00:02:14,692 --> 00:02:15,973
In the depth of the cold.

90
00:02:16,013 --> 00:02:18,176
They said afterwards the grid was four minutes

91
00:02:18,216 --> 00:02:21,123
and thirty-seven seconds from total collapse.

92
00:02:21,163 --> 00:02:23,040
A collapse that could have taken weeks to

93
00:02:23,080 --> 00:02:23,582
repair.

94
00:02:23,622 --> 00:02:24,840
The state's official count:

95
00:02:24,880 --> 00:02:26,770
two hundred forty-six people died.

96
00:02:26,810 --> 00:02:27,763
Most from the cold.

97
00:02:27,803 --> 00:02:29,635
Electricity isn't a luxury for them, sir.

98
00:02:29,675 --> 00:02:30,384
It keeps them alive.

99
00:02:30,424 --> 00:02:31,301
The last big one:

100
00:02:31,341 --> 00:02:33,133
twenty twenty-five, Spain and Portugal.

101
00:02:33,173 --> 00:02:35,354
The twenty-eighth of April, at noon, in fine

102
00:02:35,394 --> 00:02:35,827
weather.

103
00:02:35,867 --> 00:02:37,539
The voltage on the grid rose past its

104
00:02:37,579 --> 00:02:37,875
limit.

105
00:02:37,915 --> 00:02:40,132
Plants disconnected themselves to protect their equipment.

106
00:02:40,172 --> 00:02:41,433
So the voltage rose more.

107
00:02:41,473 --> 00:02:42,667
So more plants disconnected.

108
00:02:42,707 --> 00:02:45,319
In seconds, two whole countries without power.

109
00:02:45,359 --> 00:02:46,678
Tens of millions of people.

110
00:02:46,718 --> 00:02:48,238
Some places waited sixteen hours.

111
00:02:48,278 --> 00:02:49,508
The final report came out about a year

112
00:02:49,548 --> 00:02:50,013
later.

113
00:02:50,053 --> 00:02:50,470
It said:

114
00:02:50,510 --> 00:02:51,708
no single cause.

115
00:02:51,748 --> 00:02:53,546
A perfect storm of small ones.

116
00:02:53,586 --> 00:02:55,193
So after a year of investigation..

117
00:02:55,233 --> 00:02:55,904
the suspect:

118
00:02:55,944 --> 00:02:56,759
circumstances.

119
00:02:56,799 --> 00:02:58,512
The grid has enemies from outside.

120
00:02:58,552 --> 00:02:59,721
First, the sun itself.

121
00:02:59,761 --> 00:03:01,667
In nineteen eighty-nine a solar storm hit the

122
00:03:01,707 --> 00:03:04,083
Earth, and Quebec's whole grid in Canada fell

123
00:03:04,123 --> 00:03:05,165
in ninety seconds.

124
00:03:05,205 --> 00:03:06,834
Millions sat in the dark for hours.

125
00:03:06,874 --> 00:03:09,387
In eighteen fifty-nine a far bigger solar storm

126
00:03:09,427 --> 00:03:09,834
came.

127
00:03:09,874 --> 00:03:11,997
It set off sparks in telegraph offices.

128
00:03:12,037 --> 00:03:13,501
Back then they had no power grids.

129
00:03:13,541 --> 00:03:15,006
If one like it came today..

130
00:03:15,046 --> 00:03:16,293
nobody is sure what would happen.

131
00:03:16,333 --> 00:03:16,762
Second:

132
00:03:16,802 --> 00:03:17,760
humans themselves.

133
00:03:17,800 --> 00:03:20,056
December twenty fifteen, in Ukraine, people got into

134
00:03:20,096 --> 00:03:22,670
power companies' computers remotely and opened the switches

135
00:03:22,710 --> 00:03:23,684
one after another.

136
00:03:23,724 --> 00:03:25,628
About two hundred twenty-five thousand homes.

137
00:03:25,668 --> 00:03:27,493
The first known cyberattack to cut the lights.

138
00:03:27,533 --> 00:03:28,645
And third, seriously:

139
00:03:28,685 --> 00:03:29,101
the squirrel.

140
00:03:29,141 --> 00:03:31,112
A small animal climbs onto transformers and closes

141
00:03:31,152 --> 00:03:32,016
the circuit with its body.

142
00:03:32,056 --> 00:03:33,594
One of the most common causes of outages

143
00:03:33,634 --> 00:03:34,371
in North America.

144
00:03:34,411 --> 00:03:35,454
A storm-proof grid..

145
00:03:35,494 --> 00:03:37,467
felled by a half-kilo animal.

146
00:03:37,507 --> 00:03:39,008
Who also comes off badly.

147
00:03:39,048 --> 00:03:40,570
So the whole grid is down.

148
00:03:40,610 --> 00:03:41,660
How does it come back?

149
00:03:41,700 --> 00:03:43,035
Here's a neat problem:

150
00:03:43,075 --> 00:03:45,618
a power station needs electricity to start.

151
00:03:45,658 --> 00:03:47,614
Pumps, fans, computers.

152
00:03:47,654 --> 00:03:49,786
So they keep a few special stations, mostly

153
00:03:49,826 --> 00:03:51,573
dams, that can start on their own with

154
00:03:51,613 --> 00:03:52,545
nothing from outside.

155
00:03:52,585 --> 00:03:54,648
They start, wake the station next door, and

156
00:03:54,688 --> 00:03:56,571
keep growing the island piece by piece.

157
00:03:56,611 --> 00:03:58,683
Every piece must rejoin in exact step with

158
00:03:58,723 --> 00:03:59,434
the same pulse.

159
00:03:59,474 --> 00:04:01,735
That's why coming back takes hours, sometimes days.

160
00:04:01,775 --> 00:04:02,923
The fall takes seconds.

161
00:04:02,963 --> 00:04:04,090
The rise is slow.

162
00:04:04,130 --> 00:04:05,230
The human dictionary.

163
00:04:05,270 --> 00:04:06,096
Two words today.

164
00:04:06,136 --> 00:04:06,839
Blackout:

165
00:04:06,879 --> 00:04:07,722
a big outage.

166
00:04:07,762 --> 00:04:09,250
The whole grid goes down.

167
00:04:09,316 --> 00:04:10,367
And load shedding:

168
00:04:10,407 --> 00:04:11,810
cutting power in turns.

169
00:04:11,850 --> 00:04:13,390
Two hours off for one district, then the

170
00:04:13,430 --> 00:04:15,064
next, so the whole doesn't fall.

171
00:04:15,104 --> 00:04:17,203
In many countries that's not a dictionary word.

172
00:04:17,243 --> 00:04:18,316
It's a daily timetable.

173
00:04:18,356 --> 00:04:19,553
Report summary, sir.

174
00:04:19,593 --> 00:04:20,016
One:

175
00:04:20,056 --> 00:04:22,243
the grid falls like dominoes, and the protection

176
00:04:22,283 --> 00:04:23,452
that saves each piece..

177
00:04:23,492 --> 00:04:24,670
is what spreads the fall.

178
00:04:24,710 --> 00:04:25,085
Two:

179
00:04:25,125 --> 00:04:27,048
the cause can be a tree, the cold,

180
00:04:27,088 --> 00:04:29,210
the sun, a hacker, or a squirrel.

181
00:04:29,250 --> 00:04:29,673
Three:

182
00:04:29,713 --> 00:04:32,155
the fall takes seconds, the rise is slow,

183
00:04:32,195 --> 00:04:33,741
from a few stations that start on their

184
00:04:33,781 --> 00:04:34,056
own.

185
00:04:34,096 --> 00:04:34,775
And a warning:

186
00:04:34,815 --> 00:04:36,668
when their lights go out, the danger isn't

187
00:04:36,708 --> 00:04:37,446
only the dark.

188
00:04:37,486 --> 00:04:40,088
In Texas people died from fumes of generators

189
00:04:40,128 --> 00:04:41,517
and heaters run indoors.

190
00:04:41,557 --> 00:04:43,166
Next report, the last in the file:

191
00:04:43,206 --> 00:04:44,394
where is electricity going?

192
00:04:44,434 --> 00:04:46,648
Batteries the size of warehouses, sunlight that became

193
00:04:46,688 --> 00:04:48,432
their cheapest power, and who on this planet

194
00:04:48,472 --> 00:04:49,924
is still waiting for a single bulb.

195
00:04:49,964 --> 00:04:50,918
This was Earth Report.

196
00:04:50,958 --> 00:04:51,698
Agent Peek.

197
